25	\section{Coercion Resolution}
26	\seclabel{resolving-coercion}
27	For a given functional call, we first determine whether there exists a
28	declaration that is applicable without coercion.  If so, the most
29	specific declaration is selected; if not, then coercions are
30	explicitly added to the functional call.  If more than one coercion is
31	possible then the coercion that yields the most specific type is
32	chosen.  \secref{restrictions-coercion} and
33	\chapref{overloaded-declarations} describe overloading rules for the
34	declarations of coercions and overloaded functionals that guarantee
35	there exists always a most specific coercion when two or more are possible.
36	
37	We first define a notion of more specific types in the presence of coercion.
38	Recall from \secref{type-relations} that Fortress defines an
39	\emph{exclusion} relation between types which is denoted by $\excludes$.
40	
41	For types $T$ and $U$, we say that $T$ \emph{rejects} $U$,
42	and write $T \rejects U$, if for all coercions to $T$, the type coerced
43	from excludes $U$:
44	\[
45	T \rejects U \iff \forall A: A \coercedefto T \implies A \excludes U.
46	\]
47	Note that $T \rejects U$ implies $U \not\coercesto T$.
48	
49	We say that $T$ is \emph{no less specific} than $U$, and write $T
50	\morespecificeq U$, if $T$ is a subtype of $U$ or $T$ excludes, can be
51	coerced to, and rejects $U$:
52	\[
53	T \morespecificeq U \iff
54	T \Ovrsubtype U \logicor
55	(T \excludes U \logicand T \coercesto U \logicand T \rejects U).
56	\]
57	
58	The $\morespecificeq$ relation is reflexive and antisymmetric but not
59	necessarily transitive.  It is possible, for example, for
60	three types, $T$, $U$ and $V$, each excluding the other two,
61	to have coercions from $T$ to $U$ and from $U$ to $V$.  In
62	this case, $T \morespecificeq U$ and $U \morespecificeq V$ but
63	$T \not\morespecificeq V$.
64	
65	Notice that if two tuple types have a bijective correspondence
66	between their element types then the $\morespecificeq$ relationship between
67	the tuple types is equivalent to applying the $\morespecificeq$
68	relation elementwise.
69	
70	We say that $T$ is \emph{more specific} than $U$, and write $T
71	\morespecific U$, if $T \morespecificeq U \logicand T \neq U$.
72	
73	We extend the definitions of no less specific and more specific to
74	declarations.  We say that a declaration $\f(\Ps)$ is \emph{no less
75	specific} than a declaration $\f(\Qs)$ if $\Ps \morespecificeq \Qs$.
76	Similarly, we say that a declaration $\Ps_0.\f(\Ps)$ is \emph{no less
77	specific} than a declaration $\Qs_0.\f(\Qs)$ if $(\Ps_0, \Ps)
78	\morespecificeq (\Qs_0, \Qs)$.  A declaration $\f(\Ps)$ is \emph{more
79	specific} than a declaration $\f(\Qs)$ if $\Ps \morespecific \Qs$.
80	Similarly, a declaration $\Ps_0.\f(\Ps)$ is \emph{more specific} than
81	a declaration $\Qs_0.\f(\Qs)$ if $(\Ps_0, \Ps) \morespecific (\Qs_0,
82	\Qs)$.
83	
84	Now we describe how to determine which coercion is applied to a given
85	call in terms of rewriting functional calls.  The rewriting is
86	for pedagogical purposes; implementation techniques may vary.
87	
88	Consider a static call $\f(\As)$ or $\As_0.\f(\As)$.  Let $\Sigma$ be
89	the set of parameter types of functional declarations of $\f$ that are
90	applicable to the call.
91	Let $\Sigma'$ be the set of parameter types of
92	functional declarations of $\f$ that are
93	applicable with coercion to the call.
94	If $\Sigma$ is not empty then we use the overloading resolution
95	described in \secref{resolving-overloading} to determine which
96	element of $\Sigma$ is called.
97	If $\Sigma$ is empty but $\Sigma'$ is not, then we rewrite the call as
98	follows.  Define: \[\mathpzc{C} = \{T \in \Sigma' \mid S \coercedefto T
99	\logicand \As \Ovrsubtype S\}.\] Let $T \in \mathpzc{C}$ be the most
100	specific element of $\mathpzc{C}$.  In other words, there does not exist
101	$T' \in \mathpzc{C}$ such that $T' \morespecific T$.  The restrictions
102	given in \secref{restrictions-coercion} and
103	\chapref{overloaded-declarations} guarantee that such a $T$
104	exists and that it is unique.  The call $\f(\As)$ or $\As_0.\f(\As)$ is
105	rewritten to \EXP{f(\mathrm{coercion}\llbracket{T}\rrbracket(\TYP{A}))} or
106	\EXP{A_{0}.f(\mathrm{coerce}\llbracket{T}\rrbracket(A))}
107	and the declaration with parameter
108	type $T$ is applied to the call.
109	
110	As an example, consider the following definitions:
111	%trait ZZ32 excludes { ZZ64, ZZ128 } end
112	%trait ZZ64 excludes { ZZ128 }
113	%  coerce(z:ZZ32) = ...
114	%end
115	%trait ZZ128
116	%  coerce(z:ZZ32) = ...
117	%  coerce(z:ZZ64) = ...
118	%end
119	%
120	%f(z:ZZ64) = 1
121	%f(z:ZZ128) = 2
122	\begin{Fortress}
123	\(\KWD{trait} \mathbb{Z}32 \KWD{excludes} \{\,\mathbb{Z}64, \mathbb{Z}128\,\} \KWD{end}\)\\
124	\(\KWD{trait} \mathbb{Z}64 \KWD{excludes} \{\,\mathbb{Z}128\,\}\)\\
125	{\tt~~}\pushtabs\=\+\(  \KWD{coerce}(z\COLONOP\mathbb{Z}32) = \ldots\)\-\\\poptabs
126	\(\KWD{end}\)\\
127	\(\KWD{trait} \mathbb{Z}128\)\\
128	{\tt~~}\pushtabs\=\+\(  \KWD{coerce}(z\COLONOP\mathbb{Z}32) = \ldots\)\\
129	\(  \KWD{coerce}(z\COLONOP\mathbb{Z}64) = \ldots\)\-\\\poptabs
130	\(\KWD{end}\)\\[4pt]
131	\(f(z\COLONOP\mathbb{Z}64) = 1\)\\
132	\(f(z\COLONOP\mathbb{Z}128) = 2\)
133	\end{Fortress}
134	Then the call \EXP{f(\mathbb{Z}32)} resolves to the
135	declaration \EXP{f(\mathbb{Z}64)} because $\EXP{\mathbb{Z}64}
136	\morespecific \EXP{\mathbb{Z}128}$, which follows from the facts that
137	\EXP{\mathbb{Z}64} excludes \EXP{\mathbb{Z}128}, coerces to it, and rejects it:
138	the one type that coerces to \EXP{\mathbb{Z}64}, \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32}, excludes
139	\EXP{\mathbb{Z}128}.
140	The same facts make the two declarations of \VAR{f} a valid overloading, and the
141	exclusion of \EXP{\mathbb{Z}64} by \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32} does the same for the two
142	coercions to \EXP{\mathbb{Z}128} (\secref{more-specific-rule}).
143	\revision{revival-coercion-example}{The Working Draft of February 2011 declared the
144	three traits with no \KWD{excludes} clause and said that the resolution follows
145	from the fact that \EXP{\mathbb{Z}64} coerces to \EXP{\mathbb{Z}128}.
146	By the definition above, \EXP{\mathbb{Z}64} is then not more specific than
147	\EXP{\mathbb{Z}128}, and neither the two declarations of \VAR{f} nor the two
148	coercions to \EXP{\mathbb{Z}128} are a valid overloading; both implementations
149	of the revival refuse the example as the Working Draft wrote it.
150	The clauses added are spelled as \library\ spell those of their integer types,
151	each trait excluding the wider ones.}
152	
153	Notice that coercion is resolved statically.  Once a coercion is
154	statically chosen for a call, this coercion is conceptually inserted
155	into the call.
156	This means that the statically chosen coercion is applied at run time.
157	
158	For example, in the following program:
159	%trait A
160	%  coerce(c:C) = ...
161	%end
162	%trait B excludes A end
163	%trait C end
164	%object D extends {B,C} end
165	%
166	%f(a:A) = 3
167	%f(b:B) = 4
168	%
169	%c : C = D
170	%f(c)
171	\begin{Fortress}
172	\(\KWD{trait} A\)\\
173	{\tt~~}\pushtabs\=\+\(  \KWD{coerce}(c\COLONOP{}C) = \ldots\)\-\\\poptabs
174	\(\KWD{end}\)\\
175	\(\KWD{trait} B \KWD{excludes} A \KWD{end}\)\\
176	\(\KWD{trait} C \KWD{end}\)\\
177	\(\KWD{object} D \KWD{extends} \{B,C\} \KWD{end}\)\\[4pt]
178	\(f(a\COLONOP{}A) = 3\)\\
179	\(f(b\COLONOP{}B) = 4\)\\[4pt]
180	\(c \mathrel{\mathtt{:}} C = D\)\\
181	\(f(c)\)
182	\end{Fortress}
183	the call to \EXP{f(C)} resolves to the declaration
184	%\EXP{f(a\COLONOP{}A)}
185	\EXP{f(A)} despite the fact that the declaration
186	%\EXP{f(b\COLONOP{}B)}
187	\EXP{f(B)} is
188	%dynamically directly
189	applicable to the dynamic call \EXP{f(D)} and does not require
